print(len("Something I appreciate more than a lot of people is creativity, especially hands-on creativity. For me it’s not just about drawing or thinking of ideas in my head. It’s about actually making something. Using tools, building things, and seeing my ideas come to life. That’s why I really like Fessenden’s Ciongoli Center for Innovation, or the CCI. To some people it’s just a room with machines and wood, but to me it’s way more than that.I like to start with a half-finished plan. A lot of times, the plan can be abstract or completely nonsensical, but with every step, it starts to make sense and, piece by piece, becomes something tangible. I’ve taken woodworking as an elective at Fessy, and it’s so fun to turn something like a big block of wood, paired with a crude drawing on a crumpled-up piece of paper, into something useful—like the chopping board I made for my mom, a truck with detachable wheels for my little brother Jayden, and a ping pong paddle with endless splinters. I love that in woodworking, an act is basically irreversible. If I over-cut a piece, I improvise, cutting off a ring to match the proportions, and a lot of the time it’s too late to start over. I’m too deep into the hole. But that’s exactly what I love: when things go wrong, measurements are off, or pieces don’t fit, I have to think differently and try something new. I even applied this mindset to building a model maglev for a project—figuring out how to suspend the train, balance the magnets, and keep it from tipping over required the same patience and creative problem-solving, and when one setup failed, I had to improvise until it finally ran smoothly.What I really appreciate about the CCI is that it gives creativity a purpose. It’s not just making something because you have to, it’s because you want to. You get to experiment, work with other people, and actually enjoy what you’re doing. Not everyone sees how important that is, but for me, creativity is something I really value and try to use whenever I can."))